    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Vinnie Pasquantino** anchoring the offense; he claims the highest daily efficiency score at 69.49, bolstered by a significant +14% dynamic platoon advantage against Rocker. Alongside him, **Josh Naylor** (64.20) and **James Wood** (62.98) provide the core production. **James Wood** remains a priority start despite facing the high-level arm of King, as his 0.384 xwOBA and elite athleticism provide the stolen base upside we prioritize, even accounting for a slight penalty against high-hold pitchers. While we are currently waiting on official lineups, **Corbin Carroll** (59.57) remains locked in as a high-leverage asset; while the matchup against the disciplined Woo is statistically tougher, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s potential for multi-category impact outweighs the SP-skill suppression.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was leaving **Kyle Karros** out of the lineup. With a score of 53.93, he pushed for consideration due to favorable park factors and a plus-matchup against Houser. Ultimately, the optimizer opted for **Tyler Soderstrom** (53.16) in the final outfield slot; while **Kyle Karros** holds a slight edge in raw projection, **Tyler Soderstrom**\u2019s +10% park factor and middle-of-the-order potential offered a more reliable path to RBI accumulation. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Jose Caballero** (48.26); although his stadium provides a +10% boost, his position in the #9 spot and a stiff -13% platoon penalty against Ginn makes him a volatile play that we are monitoring closely as the official lineup drops.\n\nRisk management is the theme of the day, as several key spots\u2014specifically **Gabriel Moreno**, **Cole Young**, and **Austin Riley**\u2014are subject to \"Lineup Pending\" status. While **Austin Riley** (60.93) is well-positioned for a productive outing against Singer, any significant downward shift in his batting order could necessitate a re-evaluation of our bench depth. We continue to monitor the status of **Mickey Moniak**, who remains sidelined on the IL, leaving us to rely on our active corps to provide the speed-heavy profile required to gain ground in the standings. Our focus remains on maximizing plate appearances against the favorable secondary arms like Nelson and Rocker to capitalize on our projected efficiency edges.",
